\chapter{Processes, Traps, and the Illusion of a Private Machine}
\label{ch:processes}
\index{processes}\index{fork}\index{exec}\index{virtual memory}

\section{A program is not a process}

A program is a body of instructions and data in a form that can be loaded.
A process is a running environment: an address space, execution state,
credentials, open descriptors, and relationships to other processes.
Several processes can execute the same program, while one process can
replace its program image without becoming a new process.

This distinction makes the Unix process interface unusually flexible.
The shell can create a child process and arrange its environment before
asking it to execute a particular program. Redirection and pipelines do
not have to be implemented separately inside every command. They can be
established through the process's inherited resources
\parencite[ch. 8]{apue}.

The private-machine illusion is qualified. Processes share the CPU,
physical memory, filesystems, devices, and sometimes explicitly shared
memory. Their clocks reflect scheduling and contention. Resource limits
can stop growth, signals can interrupt work, and administrative action
can end execution. The operating system provides isolation and controlled
sharing, not an infinitely capable imaginary computer.

\section{Creation and replacement are separate operations}

In the familiar interface, \texttt{fork} creates a child with a closely
related initial state. Parent and child continue after the call with
different return values. Their address spaces are logically separate,
although an implementation may initially share physical pages using
copy-on-write. That optimization is common in modern systems; it is not
a requirement of the interface and should not be assumed for the supplied
xv6 implementation.

An \texttt{exec} operation replaces the current program image. It does not
create a child. A successful exec preserves the process identity while
changing the executable code, stack, and much of the memory environment.
Selected process properties survive, including descriptors not marked
close-on-exec. The exact rules belong to the specified interface and
implementation.

The separation lets a shell perform a sequence that can be described
schematically as follows:

\begin{verbatim}
create child
    in child:
        bind chosen input to descriptor 0
        bind chosen output to descriptor 1
        close unused descriptors
        execute requested program
    in parent:
        close no-longer-needed pipe ends
        wait or continue as the command requires
\end{verbatim}

This is pseudocode, not a complete C implementation. Real code must
handle failures at every stage, avoid leaking descriptors, and choose
appropriate behavior if execution fails. After fork in a multithreaded
program, the child also faces restrictions before exec because it inherits
an address space whose other threads no longer exist in that child.
An apparently simple launch can carry difficult library-state assumptions.

Other interfaces, including \texttt{posix\_spawn}, package common launch
operations differently. Their existence does not invalidate the fork/exec
model. It shows that an elegant abstraction can acquire performance and
concurrency costs in environments unlike the one that first shaped it.

\section{Entering the kernel changes authority}
\index{system calls}\index{user mode}\index{supervisor mode}

A user program cannot safely be allowed to program device hardware or
rewrite memory mappings at will. Hardware privilege levels constrain those
operations. A system call crosses into a controlled entry path where the
kernel interprets a request under the caller's credentials and the system's
resource rules.

A library function and a system call are not the same thing.
A function such as a buffered I/O routine may do substantial work in user
space and request kernel I/O only occasionally. A thin wrapper may arrange
arguments and execute the architecture's trap instruction. The boundary
that matters for protection is the controlled privilege transition, not
whether a function name appears in a familiar header.

On entry, the kernel must preserve enough register state to resume the
caller. It needs a valid kernel stack and a known mapping environment.
It must also treat user-supplied pointers as untrusted addresses, not as
permission to access arbitrary kernel memory. The requested range may
cross a page boundary, include an unmapped page, or become invalid under
concurrent changes in a more elaborate system.

The xv6 book's trap and page-table chapters make these transitions
explicit. Its trampoline arrangement allows carefully controlled code to
remain reachable across a page-table change. That is a RISC-V implementation
technique in the supplied edition, not a generic synonym for every Unix
system-call path \parencite[ch. 3--4]{xv6}.

\section{Addresses need an interpreter}

A virtual address is meaningful within a mapping context. Hardware
translates it into a physical address and checks permissions. This lets
two processes use the same numerical virtual address for unrelated data.
It also lets the kernel map the same physical page into multiple address
spaces when sharing is intentional.

In the supplied xv6 Sv39 model, ordinary pages are 4096 bytes and page
tables form a three-level tree. The address contains index fields and an
offset within a page. Walking the tree locates a page-table entry whose
physical page number and permissions determine whether the access can
proceed. Real RISC-V also constrains valid address forms; unused upper
bits are not a license to use arbitrary 64-bit values.

The translation lookaside buffer caches recent translations, reducing the
cost of repeated walks. Changing a page table may therefore require
invalidating cached translations. On multiple cores, the kernel may need
to coordinate invalidation across processors that could use the old
mapping. A memory-protection decision can become a synchronization problem.

Sixth Edition reaches a related goal through PDP-11 segmentation and
swapping arrangements. Its small virtual spaces and hardware-specific
mapping rules should be understood on their own terms. Saying that both
systems provide protected address spaces is accurate; saying that they
use the same virtual-memory implementation is not
\parencite[ch. 2, 7, 15]{lions}.

\section{Copy-on-write and faults as useful events}
\index{copy-on-write}\index{page faults}

A page fault does not always mean a programming error. It means an access
could not complete under the current translation and permission state.
A kernel can use that event to load data, allocate a page lazily, or perform
a copy-on-write operation. An invalid access can instead cause the process
to receive a signal or terminate.

For copy-on-write, two address spaces initially refer to the same physical
page with writes restricted. A write causes a fault. The kernel allocates
and copies when necessary, updates the writer's mapping, and resumes the
instruction. The process sees the logical result of independent memory
without paying to copy every page at creation time.

Correctness depends on reference counts, permissions, fault classification,
and synchronization. A page cannot be freed while another mapping still
uses it. The kernel must not mistake a genuinely read-only executable page
for a copy-on-write page. The optimization is therefore a complete
lifetime-management mechanism, not a single conditional in the fault handler.

Production virtual-memory systems add file-backed mappings, anonymous
memory, shared libraries, reclaim, swapping, huge pages, NUMA placement,
and overcommit policies. These mechanisms affect both performance and
failure behavior. A successful allocation request does not necessarily
mean every future page access will obtain physical memory under all
resource policies.

\section{Exit must leave an account of what happened}
\index{wait}\index{zombie processes}

When a process exits, most of its resources can be released promptly.
Its parent may still need an exit status and identifying information.
A zombie is the residual record kept for that purpose, not a secretly
executing program. Waiting lets a parent collect the result and permits
the remaining record to be reclaimed.

Parent-child relationships also need rules when a parent exits first.
Traditional Unix arrangements reparent orphaned children to an appropriate
system process; modern systems may have additional subreaper facilities.
The exact policy differs, but the underlying requirement remains:
termination must not leave an unreclaimable record simply because the
original parent disappeared.

This completes one traversal of the private-machine illusion.
Creation establishes identity and resources; execution replaces a program;
traps request services; mappings enforce isolation; faults support policy;
and exit leaves enough evidence for coordination. The illusion works
because the kernel remembers all these relationships even when a program
need only think about its next instruction.
